; Tutorial_3_Check_a_Word_Document.inix -- check a word document

[about]
Title=3 - Check a Word Document
Intro=Checking a Word handout before it is emailed, and reading its report: the most common use of extCheck.
Setup=A Word document of your own, such as a handout or a letter, and Microsoft Word installed.
Homework=Fix one problem the report names, check the document again with Force replacements on, and confirm that row is gone.

[step]

Say=One want: a Word handout that everyone can read, checked before I email it to a group.
Key=

[step]

Say=I open the dialog. The focus is on Source files.
Key=Alt+Control+X
Hear=extCheck dialog
Hear=Source files edit
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Rather than type the path, Browse source opens a file picker.
Key=Alt+B
Hear=Open dialog
Hear=File name edit
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=I type the start of the handout's name and press Enter. The picker closes, and its full path is in Source files.
Key=Enter
Hear=Source files edit
Hear=C colon backslash Users backslash Documents backslash Handout dot docx
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=I leave Output directory blank, so the report lands in the current folder, and I tick View output, so that folder opens when the check is done.
Key=Alt+V
Hear=View output check box, checked
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Enter starts the check. Word opens the document invisibly in the background, so nothing appears on screen.
Key=Enter
Hear=Checking Handout dot docx
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=A few seconds later the results box says what was done.
Key=
Hear=1 file checked, 6 issues
Hear=OK button
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Six issues. I press Enter, and File Explorer opens on the folder, on the new report, Handout dot csv.
Key=Enter
Hear=Handout dot csv
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=I open it in Excel. Each row is one problem, and the columns are rule, source, category, location, context, message, and remediation, which is the fix.
Key=Enter
Hear=Handout dot csv - Excel
Hear=Rule ID, A1
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Down Arrow moves to the first problem. Control with Right Arrow reads along its row.
Key=Down Arrow
Hear=Missing Alt Text, A2
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Missing alt text: a picture with no description, so a screen reader can say only that a picture is there. The location says which picture, and the remediation says how to add its description in Word.
Key=

[step]

Say=The next row is a heading problem.
Key=Down Arrow
Hear=Heading Skipped Level, A3
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=A heading skipped a level, from heading one straight to heading three. Readers who move by headings use those levels as an outline, so a gap misleads them about the structure.
Key=

[step]

Say=Others in a typical Word report: a table with no header row, a link whose text says click here rather than where it goes, and blank paragraphs used for spacing, each heard as an empty line.
Key=

[step]

Say=Each finding is a fix I can make in Word, in a minute or two: add the description, correct the heading level, mark the first table row as its header.
Key=

[step]

Say=After fixing, I check again. The old report is there, so I tick Force replacements, which replaces it rather than skipping the document.
Key=Alt+F
Hear=Force replacements check box, checked
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Enter, and the new report has fewer rows. When the count reaches zero, the handout is ready to send.
Key=Enter
Hear=1 file checked, 0 issues
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=A report can be long. In Excel, a filter on the Rule ID column shows one kind of problem at a time, which makes a long report manageable.
Key=Alt+Down Arrow
Hear=Filter, Rule ID
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.
[step]

Say=Or sort by Location, to fix the document from top to bottom in one pass.
Key=
Hear=Sort A to Z, Location
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.
[step]

Say=The Message column always says why a problem matters, not just what it is, so a fix is never a matter of guessing.
Key=
Hear=Message, A picture has no description, so a screen reader can only say that a picture is there
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.
[step]

Say=Some rules are certain, such as a picture with no description at all. Others are judgments, such as a description that may be too short to be useful; the message says which kind a finding is.
Key=
Hear=Alt Text Too Short, Picture 3
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.
[step]

Say=If Word is already open with other documents, extCheck uses its own hidden copy, and leaves yours alone.
Key=
[step]

Say=One document, checked, fixed and checked again. The next walk does the same for a slide presentation, where reading order matters most.
Key=
